      'Oxygen moves through the body by following its pressure gradient. The partial pressure of oxygen is highest in the air we breathe, slightly lower in the alveoli and arterial blood, and lowest in the tissues, where it is being used up. So oxygen moves from the air into the blood and from the blood into the tissues. Carbon dioxide goes the other way, because it is produced in the tissues: its partial pressure is highest there and lowest in the air.',
      'Getting enough oxygen to the tissues depends on a chain of steps, and a failure of any one of them causes trouble. Enough air must be breathed in (ventilation); the air and blood must be well matched in the lungs for exchange (ventilation and perfusion); the blood must be able to carry enough oxygen, which depends on haemoglobin; and the heart must pump that blood to the tissues (cardiac output).',
