      'A convenient way to describe haemoglobin’s affinity for oxygen is the P50: the partial pressure of oxygen at which haemoglobin is half saturated. In a healthy adult it is about 27 mmHg. A single number like this makes it easy to compare different conditions.',
      'The curve can shift sideways. When it moves to the right, the P50 rises: a higher partial pressure is needed to reach the same saturation, because haemoglobin’s affinity for oxygen has fallen. Haemoglobin then holds oxygen less tightly and gives it up more readily. A right shift is useful in the tissues, especially when demand is high, as in exercise, or when the supply is poor, as after severe blood loss.',
